  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate Finance: Systematic Observations Financing Facility (SOFF - early warning systems)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The United Nations&#x2019; Early Warnings for All initiative, launched by UN Secretary-General Ant&#xf3;nio Guterres in 2022, aims to ensure that every person on Earth is protected by early warning systems against extreme weather events. Early warning systems are a highly cost-effective tool for climate adaptation and disaster risk reduction, enabling governments and communities to anticipate and respond to hazards such as storms, floods, droughts, and heatwaves. However, nearly half of the world&#x2019;s countries&#x2014;particularly Least Developed Countries (LDCs) and Small Island Developing States (SIDS)&#x2014;still lack adequate coverage due to major gaps in weather and climate observation capabilities.
The Systematic Observations Financing Facility (SOFF) addresses this critical challenge by strengthening the collection and exchange of high-quality weather and climate data, which form the foundation of effective forecasting and early warning systems. Many developing countries currently provide only a small share of the observational data required under the World Meteorological Organization&#x2019;s Global Basic Observing Network (GBON) standards. Recognizing weather and climate observations as a global public good, SOFF was established by the WMO, UNDP, and UNEP as a dedicated financing mechanism to help countries close these data gaps. By focusing on the first and most critical link in the early warning chain&#x2014;systematic observation&#x2014;SOFF serves as a cornerstone of the Early Warnings for All initiative.
SOFF has a unique role within the climate finance architecture by providing long-term grant financing, technical assistance, and capacity building to strengthen national meteorological and hydrological services in developing countries. Through partnerships with advanced meteorological services, multilateral development banks, and UN agencies, SOFF promotes sustainable and lasting investments, including the operation and maintenance of observation infrastructure. By addressing urgent needs while building durable institutional capacity, SOFF contributes to more reliable forecasts, improved climate resilience, and better protection of vulnerable populations from climate-related disasters.</narrative>
  </description>
